# 0608 — SERIES 600 UMBRELLA OS MODEL

## Status
LOCKED. This document governs every module, engine, and interface decision
in the LEGAiSEE Command Center. If a future build (by Claude, Windsurf, GPT,
Kimi, or anyone else) conflicts with this document, this document wins.
No module spec, work order, or patch supersedes it without an explicit
architectural ruling logged here.

## The mistake this document corrects
Every prior session — including multiple sessions with Claude — defaulted
to "fix this module" thinking. A bug gets diagnosed, a file gets patched,
the immediate symptom clears, and the next module is built the same
disconnected way. The system grew to 70+ PHP files, four competing
dashboards, and a duplicated Project Manager app because every build
decision was made at the file level instead of the system level.

This document exists so that never happens again. Every decision from
here forward gets checked against the whole system first, the single file
second.

## 1. The business is four departments, not four products

LEGAiSEE is one business with four departments:

1. **Business Archaeology** — excavate a client's old marketing, data, and
   history; translate it into a modern authority system.
2. **AI Agent** — analyze ingested artifacts and chats, build the node
   graph, suggest improvements, maintain persistent project memory.
3. **Project Manager** — John's own daily interface for running the
   business: tasks, calls, meetings.
4. **Knowledge System** — ongoing monitoring of onboarded clients;
   marketing, analytics, node tracking, monthly adjustments. Long-term
   goal: a full digital copy of the live business, all departments,
   not just marketing.

Every file in the tree belongs to one of these four departments.
`ProjectManager/` and `pm_module.php` are two failed attempts at
department 3 — one gets archived, not both maintained.

## 2. The money pipeline (department 1, the revenue path)

Prospect → Excavation → Dig Review → Dossier Production → Invoice.

As of this document, stage 5 does not exist anywhere in the codebase.
No module, engine, or table handles invoicing or payment. This is the
single highest-priority gap in the entire system — nothing upstream of
it matters until money can actually be collected at the end of it.

Client pipeline stage must become **one real field**, written by every
module that touches it, read by every dashboard that displays it.
Today it is scattered and re-derived independently by each module
(this is what broke `cockpit_module.php`). One field, one source of
truth, is a prerequisite for every UI item in this document.

## 3. Two interface layers, not one

**Operator Dashboard** — for staff. Shows the money pipeline: today's
tasks, every client's current stage, flags that need action. This is
what loads when anyone who is not John logs in.

**Owner Console** — for John. Contains the Operator Dashboard as one
entry point, plus:
- Universal entity search (type a business name, get everything)
- Node graph explorer with plain-English explanations
- Direct view/edit/delete access to every subsystem's data
- A literal file tree browser
- Ingest intake
- Database access
- System health monitoring

The Owner Console is the umbrella. Every existing and future module is
a subsystem accessed *through* it — never a standalone page reached by
its own separate nav link, the way the current 30-button Grand Lobby
works.

## 4. The core interaction law: what, connections, why — never how

This is the rule that applies to every subsystem, not just the node
graph. For anything the system computes or generates automatically,
the interface shows three things by default:

1. **What exists** (the fact, record, or artifact)
2. **What it's connected to** (relationships, not foreign keys)
3. **Why it matters to the business today** (plain English)

The mechanism — how a node was scored, how an artifact was classified,
how a recommendation was generated, how a database row was computed —
stays hidden by default. It is always available one click deeper, for
when John is actually modifying that specific piece. It is never the
default view.

This law applies identically to:
- **Extended memory / reasoning engines** — show what the system
  remembers and why it's surfacing it, not the arbitration math.
- **Ingest system** — paste anything in (chat logs, artifacts, emails);
  the system classifies it and routes it automatically. The
  classification logic is invisible; the result ("this supports client
  X's authority claim about Y") is not.
- **Database** — business-object view by default (this client, this
  case, this task, shown as relationships). Raw table/schema view stays
  one click away, always available, never removed.
- **File tree browser** — a literal OS-style file browser (Explorer/
  Finder equivalent) over the real server tree, plus two extra powers:
  a notes system attachable to any folder, and the ability to drop a
  file directly into a client's folder without routing it through
  ingest. This is a power tool for direct intervention, not the default
  client view.
- **Client detail page** (opened from the client list) — files and
  data grouped by what they are *to the business* ("Acme's excavated
  2002 TV reel," "Acme's current authority dossier draft," "Acme's dig
  review queue"), plain-English one-liner per item, raw path and code
  one click deeper. This doubles as v1 of the future client-facing
  login dashboard.
- **Home dashboard** — plain English on what matters right now across
  the whole business, plus literal system health: storage quota,
  backup status, maintenance flags.

## 5. Scope requirement: replace cPanel, FTP, and phpMyAdmin

If a normal OS file manager, database tool, or hosting control panel
can do something for John, the Owner Console must be able to do it
natively. The target: John never logs into cPanel, never opens FTP,
never opens phpMyAdmin again. Every file operation, every database
edit, every system health check happens inside the system itself.

## 6. What this document does not do

This is not a build order and not a module spec. It does not say which
file gets written first. Its only job is to be the filter every future
build decision passes through: *does this change serve the whole
system as described here, or does it solve one file in isolation.*
Anything that only does the latter gets rejected regardless of how
good the individual fix is.

